The atom-slot-keys class-name format (`_a_`, see
`Class_format`/`Hash_class.slot_class`) hashes its value only within one
(context, family[, mask]) bucket, not globally - so two atoms that
coincidentally land on the same class name but render different CSS must
be a hard build error, the same way two different `_id_` identities
colliding on the same hash already are (see identity-collision.t).

  $ cat > a.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ("._a_abchgt{color:red;}", "_a_abchgt", "0,1,0 own", "color", 1, false)]
  > EOF

  $ cat > b.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ("._a_abchgt{color:blue;}", "_a_abchgt", "0,1,0 own", "color", 1, false)]
  > EOF

  $ styled-ppx.generate a.ml b.ml
  styled-ppx: atom class collision: "._a_abchgt{color:red;}" and "._a_abchgt{color:blue;}" both use class "_a_abchgt" but render different CSS. The class ends in a 4-character base-36 hash of the declaration (1679616 values per context, family and mask), so two different declarations can share it by chance. Write one of the two declarations differently (an equivalent value or unit), and report the pair at https://github.com/davesnx/styled-ppx/issues.
  [1]

Same class, same content (the ordinary "same module compiled twice" case -
native + melange `copy_files`, vendoring) is not a collision: it dedups to
one rule like any other, same as today.

  $ cat > e.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ("._a_same{color:green;}", "_a_same", "0,1,0 own", "color", 1, false)]
  > EOF

  $ cat > f.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ("._a_same{color:green;}", "_a_same", "0,1,0 own", "color", 1, false)]
  > EOF

  $ styled-ppx.generate e.ml f.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  ._a_same{color:green;}

A `_id_` (identity class) collision is a different, pre-existing check
(identity-collision.t) - this one only looks at the class of an atom rule's
key, so a `_id_` clash in rules without a key doesn't trip it.

  $ cat > g.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css "._id_shared{color:red;}"]
  > EOF

  $ cat > h.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css "._id_shared{color:blue;}"]
  > EOF

  $ styled-ppx.generate g.ml h.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  ._id_shared{color:red;}
  ._id_shared{color:blue;}

A `_in_` (interpolation-bundle) class sharing its name across different
content is NOT a collision - `Css_file.re`'s bundling already, legitimately,
puts several different declarations from one binding under one shared
class when they all carry a `$(...)` interpolation (this is exactly the
real shape `minify-interpolation.t` and the `reason-cx-*` snapshot tests
depend on - see the plan's "Collision check" notes for why the OLD,
unconditional version of this check broke them). The collision check must
recognize the `_in_` prefix and skip it entirely, in both directions.

  $ cat > i.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ("._in_bundled{margin:var(--mySize-1);}", "_in_bundled", "0,1,0 own", "margin", 15, false)]
  > EOF

  $ cat > j.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ("._in_bundled{padding:var(--mySize-2);}", "_in_bundled", "0,1,0 own", "padding", 15, false)]
  > EOF

  $ styled-ppx.generate i.ml j.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  ._in_bundled{margin:var(--mySize-1);}
  ._in_bundled{padding:var(--mySize-2);}
